  content_json = '{"prepare":"Quiet location? No distractions? Talk aids ready?","reason":"Statistics show that working during winter conditions and cold weather increases the risk of accidents and illness.","why":"To prevent cold stress, hypothermia, frostbite, and slips on ice or snow.","outline":"This TBT covers cold weather safety, PPE, recognising hypothermia and frostbite, and reducing winter risks.","sections":[{"title":"Cold Weather Safety Instructions","body":"alcohol and drug abuse are happen on this site.","points":[]},{"title":"Cold Weather Safety","body":"Now that the winter season approaches employees are at risk of sustaining injuries due to a slip, effects of Alcohol and Drugs on trips or falls or of suffering ill health. During cold weather, it can have a chilling effect on the your safety and the safety of senses to see, smell, and feel. It is usually difficult t o be productive when you are cold. Accident others. statistics prove that there is an increased risk of personal injury during the winter months. Typical","points":[]},{"title":"7) working at height","body":"When working in construction, being outside for long periods is part of the job. During the winter months, outdoor work can really be a challenge – as sometimes, temperatures can get quite low. These types of working conditions can lead to cold stress. There’s usually a lot more wind which can take heat away from your body. Snow and rain can make your clothing wet as well. Cold stress usually happens slowly over the course of a day – and workers may not even realize they have been affected by the cold weather. ( Until it’s too late) When the weather is colder, we have to use more energy to stay warm and this is further amplified when it is windy, raining or snowing; your body loses heat even faster now. At an air temperature of 10°C, if the wind speed is 20mph, the effective temperatu re is 0°C. There is no specific temperature stated by law when it comes to construction work however much of the law refers to temperatures as ‘comfortable.’","points":[]},{"title":"Q: What could happen ?","body":"Hypothermia - Being cold over a prolonged period of time can cause a drop in body temperature. If your body temperature drops below 35°C you can be at risk of hypothermia. Hypothermia is a condition in which the body uses up its stored energy and can no longer produce heat. Often occurs after prolonged exposure to cold temperature. handling, particularly if the person is unconscious, gently remove wet clothing, Warm the person gradually and slowly, using available sources of heat . Warning it can progress to a life - threatening condition where shivering stops, or the person loses consciousness; and cardiac arrest may occur. Frostbite - A more severe condition, where both the skin and the underlying tissue (fat, muscle, bone) are frozen. Skin appears white/grey and waxy and is hard to the touch. No sensation – the area is numb or tingling. Frostbite most often affects the nose, ears, cheeks, chin, fingers, or toes. Frostbite can be serious and can result in amputation. Get medical help. area until you can ensure it will stay warm, Warm the area gradually; use body heat, or warm water Avoid direct heat which can burn the skin. Hypothermia - Being cold over a prolonged period of time can cause a drop in body temperature. If your body temperature drops below 35°C you can be at risk of hypothermia. Hypothermia is a condition in which the body uses up its stored energy and can no longer produce heat. Often occurs after prolonged exposure to cold temperature. handling, particularly if the person is unconscious, gently remove wet clothing, Warm the person gradually and slowly, using available sources of heat. Warning it can progress to a life - threatening condition where shivering stops, or the person loses consciousness; and cardiac arrest may occur. Frostbite - A more severe condition, where both the skin and the underlying tissue (fat, muscle, bone) are frozen. Skin appears white/grey and waxy and is hard to the touch. No sensation – the area is numb or tingling. Frostbite most often affects the nose, ears, cheeks, chin, fingers, or toes. Frostbite can be serious and can result in amputation. Get medical help. area until you can ensure it will stay warm, Warm the area gradually; use body heat, or warm water Avoid direct heat which can burn the skin.","points":["Early signs: Shivering, fatigue, loss of coordination, Confusion, Slurred speech","First Aid: Move the person to a warm place, Lay the person down and avoid rough","Symptoms: Reduced blood flow to hands/feet, numbness, aching, tingling/stinging","First - Aid: Move to a warm place, do not rub, or massage the area, do not warm the","Early signs: Shivering, fatigue, loss of coordination, Confusion, Slurred speech","First Aid: Move the person to a warm place, Lay the person down and avoid rough","Symptoms: Reduced blood flow to hands/feet, numbness, aching, tingling/stinging","First - Aid: Move to a warm place, do not rub, or massage the area, do not warm the"]},{"title":"Personal Protective Equipment","body":"PPE designed to provide warmth such as hard hat liners should be used but ensure any PPE provided is compatible and does not cause other risks. Avondale will provide high - vis waterproof jackets if working outside in winter conditions; this is in replacemen t of a high - vis vest. somewhat tighter (to trap body heat). more effective. wet. Once wet, they will not serve as good protection from the cold. loss comes from the head. the effects of the cold first. Wool socks help, but 4 - buckle overshoes can provide better protection. gloves will provide the best protection.","points":["Always dress in layers with the outer layers being rather loose and the inner layers being","Use the outer layer of clothing as a windbreaker. This will make the layers underneath","Minimize sweat. If you begin to get hot, take a layer off. Try to avoid getting your clothing","Wear head protection. This will increase your overall warmth. Over half of the body’s heat","Be sure to properly protect your feet. Unless you are moving around, your feet will feel","Gloves are very important. Most often a thin pair of wool gloves under a pair of leather"]},{"title":"Slips & Trips","body":"To r educe the risk of slips on ice, frost, or snow, you need to assess the risk and put in a system to manage it. Identify the outdoor areas used by pedestrians /employees are most likely to be affected by ice, for example: - building entrances, car parks, pedestrian walkways, shortcuts, sloped areas and areas constantly in the shade or wet. Grit/Salt can be used to prepare for these conditions. Keeping up to date on the weather forecast to act against freezing materials.","points":[]},{"title":"Reducing Risk","body":"effective method of ensuring better recovery and efficiency. calories and nutrients to give you the fuel you need. Always start with a hearty breakfast. accelerated heat loss and impair movement. winter takes lots of energy. Be sure to pace yourself.","points":["Regular breaks being taken in a warm building with access to warm drinks, is an","Make sure you eat well. This does not mean a high fat diet, but one with enough","Wet clothing should be removed as quickly as possible as it can cause both","Get plenty of rest. Working in the cold and even travelling to and from work in the","Always dress properly for cold weather.","Put on warm clothes before you go outside.","Carry extra dry clothing if you are likely to get wet.","Keep your skin dry. Wet skin freezes quicker than dry skin.","Drink plenty of water to prevent dehydration.","If possible, do outdoor work during the warmest part of the day.","Avoid sitting still outdoors for long periods of time.","Take adequate breaks from the cold.","Do not touch metal or wear metal jewelry outdoors in the cold.","Avoid alcohol, cigarettes and too much coffee and other drinks with caffeine.","Stay in good physical